# Model weights & checkpoints
*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.h5
*.onnx
*.pb

# Large data files
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.zip
*.npy
*.npz
processed_data/

# Logs
*.log
wandb/
runs/
logs/
outputs/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local cache of the large cloud-hosted files (by default ../archive/, outside the repo;
# this rule is here to stop anyone dropping them inside it)
archive/
*.tar

# Exception: the restorer itself must be committed. A fresh clone has an empty archive/ and
# the packages have to come down from cloud storage; if the script were not in the repo,
# someone holding an 8.5 G tar would have no idea where to unpack it. git does not descend
# into an ignored directory, so the path has to be re-admitted level by level.
!archive/
archive/*
!archive/restore.sh
!archive/*.manifest

# folders
# WARNING: .gitignore does **not** support end-of-line comments -- a comment must sit on its
# own line; placed after a pattern it becomes part of the pattern
# Generated by setup.sh; records where the environment lives, different for each person
.env.local
# Third-party solver, cloned on demand by setup.sh --funwave (see README section 1.4)
FUNWAVE-TVD/
server_info
legacy/env_info
# Outputs (train/ vis/ web/) never enter the repo -- for the one exception see below
results/*

# Exception: the **default weights** for web-demo must be committed (best.pt 8.6 M plus 3
# .hydra yamls). Otherwise a fresh clone can start the demo but the dropdown is empty and
# nothing can be clicked -- and the whole point of this demo is "open it and it works".
# git does not descend into an ignored directory, so the path is re-admitted level by level;
# the trailing ! must come after the *.pt near the top of the file in order to override it.
!results/web/
results/web/*
!results/web/model/
!results/web/model/**
# But not .hydra/hydra.yaml -- no code reads it, and it holds the absolute paths hydra
# recorded (/nfs/hpc/share/<someone>/...), which would carry a username into the repo.
# config.yaml uses the ${repo:} resolver and does not have this problem.
results/web/model/**/.hydra/hydra.yaml
# 306 mp4 files (486 M). They had travelled with the repo ever since the hpm/vis/ days
# (commit d8c9d1c), and those 482 M in .git were essentially all of them -- contradicting the
# statement at the top of archives.tsv that videos do not enter the repo.
# Pulled out 2026-08-22: the files moved to ../archive/legacy/hpm/vis/ outside the repo, and
# the cloud copy is archive/legacy_hpm_vis_20260820.tar (334 files, md5-verified one by one).
# NOTE: this only stops further growth -- the objects remain in history, clone size is
# unchanged, and a real slim-down would mean rewriting history.
legacy/hpm/vis/
data/*

# Exception: the **inputs** of the FUNWAVE reference case TK94 must be committed (input.txt
# and gauges.txt, the only record of what was actually run). The output (8.5 G), the binaries
# and LOG.txt stay excluded. The wildcard form is kept deliberately -- when make_cases.py
# creates new cases later, their inputs get tracked automatically.
!data/fwv/
data/fwv/*
!data/fwv/*/
data/fwv/*/*
!data/fwv/*/input.txt
!data/fwv/*/gauges.txt
!data/fwv/make_cases.py
!data/fwv/wk_check.py

# Same reasoning: the generation scripts for the 3D data must be committed (the 254 G of data
# itself stays out, but "how it was produced" has to survive)
!data/3d/
data/3d/*
!data/3d/crop_fields.py
!data/3d/crop_fields.sh
vis_*/
vis/

# -- web-demo (code/web-demo/) --
# These rules used to be scattered across code/web-demo/.gitignore; merged into this single
# file on 2026-08-23. The downside of two locations was measured: the two node_modules rules
# were written in both places (the deeper file wins, so the one in the root was dead), while
# the server/bindings/ rule never took effect at all because it carried an **end-of-line
# comment** -- see the warning near the top of this file.
code/web-demo/server/target/*
# Exception: the compiled backend binary **must be committed** (4.5 M on disk, about 1.6 M in
# git) -- a fresh clone can run ./start.sh straight away (verified: no build, no conda, the
# frontend is baked into the binary). With unchanged sources a rebuild is byte-for-byte
# reproducible, so it produces no phantom diffs. NOTE: after changing server/src/, remember to
# rebuild before committing. git does not descend into an ignored directory, so the path is
# re-admitted level by level.
!code/web-demo/server/target/release/
code/web-demo/server/target/release/*
!code/web-demo/server/target/release/wave-demo
# Where ts-rs drops its files during cargo test; the real artifacts live in web/src/types/
code/web-demo/server/bindings/
code/web-demo/web/node_modules/
code/web-demo/web/*.tsbuildinfo
# NOTE: code/web-demo/web/dist/ **must be committed** (the frontend cannot be built on a login
# node -- the 15 GB ulimit -v is a hard limit and vue-tsc's WASM will not start), so do not add
# a rule here that excludes it.

# Runtime state of web-demo (results/web/: copies of the weights, intermediate fields, rendered
# output, submission records, logs, pid) is already excluded by the results/ rule above. The
# whole directory is generated at run time and has to be created by hand on first deployment;
# see code/web-demo/README.md.

# -- Rendered output of the fwv line must be committed --------------------------------------
# Decided 2026-08-23: the 40 mp4 files (55 M) under `legacy/hpm/fwv/` go into the repo. They
# were in neither git nor any package -- the only unique copies anywhere in the tree; once
# committed, everything has a home.
# The 9 small videos in the sibling vis_align/ were tracked already, so this also makes the
# treatment of evidence-grade output consistent.
#
# NOTE, to be clear about the cost: mp4 files are not delta-compressed, so these 55 M are added
# to .git verbatim and every clone has to pull them. filter-repo only rewrites history and
# cannot touch these (they live in new commits).
#
# Re-admitted level by level: vis/ is a global wildcard (see above), so this directory has to
# be ! back in first.
!legacy/hpm/fwv/vis/
# hpm_fw_ss_R4/ used to have a dedicated exclude rule; it was removed above, so nothing is
# needed here any more

# -- Directory skeleton ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The contents all live in the packages (see archives.tsv), but **the directories themselves
# must be committed** -- a fresh clone then shows at a glance where things belong, instead of
# leaving someone to guess at an empty repo. git does not track empty directories, so each
# placeholder holds a .gitkeep saying which package its contents are in and how to restore it.
#
# The form has to re-admit level by level: git does not descend into an excluded directory, so
# the parent is ! back in first, then its contents are excluded, then .gitkeep alone is
# re-admitted. This whole block sits at the end of the file deliberately -- wildcards such as
# vis/ and outputs/ appear earlier, and git lets **the last matching rule win**, so placing it
# earlier would let them override it.
#
# NOTE: the "already in place" test in both archive/restore.sh and setup.sh skips .gitkeep.
# Without that, the skeleton alone would make every package look already restored, and nobody
# would be warned that the data was never unpacked (this trap has been hit once).
!data/2d/
data/2d/*
!data/2d/.gitkeep
!data/3d/cropped_0.05/
data/3d/cropped_0.05/*
!data/3d/cropped_0.05/.gitkeep
!data/fwv/*/output/
data/fwv/*/output/*
!data/fwv/*/output/.gitkeep
!results/train/
results/train/*
!results/train/.gitkeep
!results/vis/
results/vis/*
!results/vis/.gitkeep
!results/web/priors/
results/web/priors/*
!results/web/priors/.gitkeep
!results/web/vis/
results/web/vis/*
!results/web/vis/.gitkeep
!legacy/hpm/vis/
legacy/hpm/vis/*
!legacy/hpm/vis/.gitkeep
!legacy/hpm/outputs/
legacy/hpm/outputs/*
!legacy/hpm/outputs/.gitkeep
